   "InfoDisplay_37010004_Title": "A Family Photo",
   "InfoDisplay_37010005_Text": "To my wife, Mingxi,\n\nI hope this letter finds you well. The paperwork has piled high of late, and I have not written back in time. Forgive me for leaving you to worry. I am in good health.\n\nThe Tacet Discord Outbreaks along the border have grown worse, and there have been some transfers within the Ministry. One of them... concerns me, though it is still under discussion. If it is settled, <s>I am to be stationed in the Land of Xuanfang</s> and I will be even farther from you than I am now... As for where I am bound, and when I set out, I am not yet at liberty to write...\n\n(The letter breaks off here, leaving only a few blots of ink, as though the writer had held his brush poised for a long while and, in the end, set it down without going on.)",
   "InfoDisplay_37010005_Title": "An Incomplete Letter",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000001_Text": "Station Name                             Arrival Time                            Duration\n\nStation of the Lost            Listen for announcement       Listen for announcement\n\nStation of Choice              Listen for announcement       Listen for announcement\n\nStation of Letters Home    Listen for announcement       Listen for announcement\n\nStation of Homeland         Listen for announcement       Listen for announcement\n\nStation of Birthday            Listen for announcement       Listen for announcement\n\n...",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000001_Title": "Train Transition Board",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000002_Text": "Patient: JLB-2391 | Rank: Corporal\nPhysical: Gunshot wound (penetrating, left forearm)\nPsychological: Severe aversion to firearms\n...\n \nPatient: JLB-3562 | Rank: Sergeant\nPhysical: Burn injuries\nPsychological: Insomnia; severe depression\n...\n \nPatient: JLB-9248 | Rank: Lieutenant\nPhysical: Mild concussion (under observation)\nPsychological: Hypersensitivity to noises\n...\n \nInspector: Lily",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000002_Title": "Warzone Casualty Report",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000003_Text": "April 17th. Rain and gray skies. The military began its private inquiry into my conduct today.\nThe military began its private inquiry into my conduct today. I thought I could protect them... but in the end, I failed to protect anyone.\nThe military found me guilty and shipped me off to the Fifth Sector... Fear, anger, sorrow... I feel none of them. I'm too weary to think. All I can do now is stay ready to save the soldiers on the front line.",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000003_Title": "Diary",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000004_Text": "November 10th. Clear skies. The Fifth Sector. The night before the assault...\nIt was such a quiet night. So quiet that, for a moment, all of us could almost believe the war had never happened.",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000004_Title": "Diary",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000005_Text": "November 11th. A day I will never forget...\nEven now, I remember every single cry they let out.\nEven now, I remember... the feel of every raindrop against my face.",
+  "InfoDisplay_38000005_Title": "Diary",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010001_Text": "Patient ID         Sector        Primary Injuries\nLB-2391            Third          Gunshot wound, penetrating wound to the left forearm, psychological aversion to firearms\nLB-3562            Seventh     Burns, insomnia, severe depression\nLB-9248            Second      Mild concussion, severe psychological aversion to loud noises\nLB-2584            Sixth          Broken femur, irritability and aggression, severe depression\n\n\nSigned and confirmed: Lily",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010001_Title": "Warzone Casualty Report",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010002_Text": "Patient ID: LB-4671\nAge: 22\nGender: Male\nSector: Fourth\nPre-War Occupation: ▇▇ ▇▇ \nWard: 6F-08, Cot 1, Private Room\n\nPreliminary Diagnosis: Acute stress disorder, primarily presenting as acute anxiety, hysterical mutism, and insomnia.\n\nThe patient was subjected to enemy fire while on night patrol in the Fourth Sector. The shelter he sought collapsed under the bombardment, burying him for approximately four hours before other soldiers rescued him. After being rescued, the patient was disoriented, suffering violent tremors throughout his body and unable to stand.\n\nInitial Treatment Plan:\nPrivate-room rest and observation; minimize visual and auditory stimuli.\nAdminister sedatives when necessary.\nA psychiatrist to speak with the patient for 30 minutes each day and record his psychological condition.\n\nKey Notes (For Physician's Personal Use):\nOctober 3\nPatient transferred to this hospital.\n\nOctober 17\nPatient spoke voluntarily for the first time. It was only a short sentence, but it's a good start. I hope he recovers soon.\n\nOctober 20\nDuring rounds, I found the patient had already been discharged from the hospital. Several officers were helping him complete the discharge procedures. Could he really have recovered in such a short time?",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010002_Title": "Diagnostic Report (I)",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010003_Text": "Patient ID: LB-6267\nAge: 18\nGender: Male\nSector: Sixth\nPre-War Occupation: ▇▇ ▇▇ \nWard: 10F-05, Cot 2, Four-Bed Room\n\nPreliminary Diagnosis: Acute stress disorder, irritability, and suicidal tendencies.\n\nThe patient was caught in a surprise attack in the Sixth Sector and was the sole survivor of his squad. Examination found no obvious external wounds or fractures, yet the patient repeatedly claimed to have extensive wounds all over his body and showed signs of discomfort. He was conscious and able to state his name and military ID accurately, but showed mild confusion about time and location. When asked about what happened on the battlefield, he became highly agitated and displayed intense aversion to others.\n\nInitial Treatment Plan:\nRest in a quiet environment and minimize visual and auditory stimuli.\nAdminister sedatives when necessary.\nGradually increase physical activity to prevent muscle atrophy.\nA psychiatrist to conduct motivational conversations for no more than 30 minutes each day to help stabilize the patient's emotions.\n\nKey Notes (For Physician's Personal Use):\nJune 7\nPatient transferred to this hospital.\n\nJune 10\nThe patient trembled violently throughout the night, muttering to himself. Even after sedation, he could not sleep and insisted on keeping the lights on with someone beside him. To avoid disturbing other patients in the room, he had to be temporarily moved to a private room.\n\nJune 11\nDuring today's conversation, the patient showed considerable initiative and shared many fond memories from his school days. Remembering happy things can be a form of self-healing. I believe this is a good start.\n\nJune 12\nThe patient killed himself. The officers sealed off the room. When I saw him again, he was wrapped tightly in a black plastic bag.\nHow did it come to this? Was he transferred to a private room just so he could kill himself? He was reminiscing about his school days only yesterday. So why...?",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010003_Title": "Diagnostic Report (II)",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010004_Text": "To my ten-year-old Liam,\n\nHappy tenth birthday, Liam! Have you learned the lullaby I taught you yet? If you have, don't get too proud of yourself! There are still so, so many songs for you to learn. You'll only get better with practice. One day, you'll even play better than me!\nOh, and now that you're ten, it's time for my little man to get used to sleeping on his own. Don't worry, though. I will always be right there beside you, watching over you quietly, just like the moon.\n\nTo my twenty-year-old Liam,\n\nHappy birthday, Liam. You're a young man of twenty now. I wonder, have you found a girl you like yet?\nI'm afraid I can't give you much advice when it comes to love. But whoever she is, you can be sure I'll be rooting for you!\n\nTo my thirty-year-old Liam,\n\nLiam, you're a grown man of thirty now. Did you marry the girl you love? Or perhaps... you've already become a father yourself!\nIf you've got a little one of your own, I'm sure they've inherited your adorable side. Just make sure they don't inherit your mischievous streak, too!\nIf you've made it this far, I bet you're chuckling to yourself right now. \"Mama still treats me like a little child.\"\nWell, here's something you should know, sweetheart. You'll always be my boy. Ten, twenty, thirty... no matter how old you grow, my love for you will never fade.\n\nTo my forty-year-old Liam,\n\nMy dear Liam, how time flies. In the blink of an eye, you've grown older than your own mother.\nYou've worked so hard through all these years, from ten to twenty, from twenty to thirty, and now here you are. You've done wonderfully. So hold on tight to the happiness you've found. Carry it with you today, tomorrow, and every day after that. Keep it close to your heart, and live your life to the fullest.\n...\n...\nTo Liam, my final letter,\n\nAs much as I hate to write this, by the time you read this letter, you may be nearing the end of your life. Congratulations, my dear boy. You've lived a long, full life. A life worth living. To face death is frightening, and you must be very afraid. I know that feeling, sweetheart. And it breaks my heart to know I won't be there beside you. I won't be able to sit at your bedside and hold your hand when you're hurting, the way I did when you were little. The only thing I can do is leave you these words: Don't be afraid, my child. Let us think of death as the beginning of another journey.",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010004_Title": "Letter to Liam",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010005_Text": "To whoever is reading this letter:\n\nThank you for finding this letter, and forgive my lack of manners for not knowing how to address you. Perhaps you're someone I know, or perhaps we've never met. I don't know why I'm writing this. Is it because I know I'm about to die? Or because I want to confess my sins...? I don't know...\n\nI remember it was autumn. The orator's voice echoed through the hall, a dry spark setting a silent room ablaze. He kept saying that war was the Federated Cities' greatest instrument of change. That our generation had grown too clean, too soft. That we needed to be tempered in blood.\n\nAt the time, I was a musician. I had always believed beauty was something gentle and orderly. But the orator's words tore apart everything I thought I knew about it. For a moment, I couldn't make sense of what I was feeling. I mistook those complicated emotions for excitement and wrote my name on the enlistment form. I told my family and my friends. My mother slapped me hard across the face, tears in her eyes, and said, \"Your hands are meant to play the piano, not hold a gun and hurt people!\" But by then, I had already been swept up in the fervor. I thought my mother's words were exactly the kind of weakness the orator had warned us about.\n\nLater, I joined the army as a medic. We boarded a train singing while girls threw flowers at us as we passed. As the train pulled away, I truly felt like a great composer setting out to conduct some magnificent symphony.\n\nBut... the front was nothing like I had imagined.\n\nDuring the first artillery barrage, I cowered in a shell crater like a dog caught in the rain. There was none of the noble strength the orator had promised. There was only fear, cold, and the maddening feeling of my boots sinking into the mud, with no way to pull them free.\n\nI don't remember exactly when or where I fired my first shot. But I remember the first man I killed. I was tending to a wounded soldier when he lunged at me. I drew the sidearm at my waist on instinct and fired. After the shot, the world seemed to go quiet.\n\nI pried open his hand. Two things fell from his pocket: a little sketchbook and a photograph of a woman and a child.\n\nI opened the sketchbook. It contained drawings of fields, cottages in the countryside, rivers that seemed to stretch on forever, and a carefully rendered self-portrait. He was just like me. He had an artist's hands, too.\n\nIn that moment, I suddenly felt as if something had been torn out of me. I wanted to return to that hall, walk onto the stage, and clamp my hands over the orator's mouth. I wanted to return to that hall, find that young man named Grimm, and tear up his enlistment form. I wanted to return to the Federated Cities as they once were, with ordinary people and Resonators sitting beneath the same tree—you drawing, me playing the piano—instead of taking aim at each other's hearts in the ruins.\n\nBut nothing will ever return to the way it was. Even if the war ends, we can never go back. No one will forgive me, and no one will forgive our generation.\n\nGrimm",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010005_Title": "The Last Letter",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010006_Text": "Since March 21, the government forces of the Federated Cities and the Resonator army have been locked in battle across this city for three months. The once-celebrated \"City of Progress\" has been torn apart by relentless artillery fire. \n\nOur reporters traveled along the highway toward the front. Everywhere they looked, ammunition crates, stretchers, and wrecked vehicles lined the roadside. Photography was strictly forbidden. All they could leave behind was this written account:\n\nWe saw... shell craters riddling the streets. Soldiers huddled in muddy trenches. Men and women pushed to the brink of mental collapse by exhaustion and fear. Medics moved through the smoke, braving incoming fire to carry the wounded away from the front. Farther ahead, we found ourselves at a loss for words. Behind us, artillery rumbled like distant thunder. It was broad daylight, yet the flames had stained the sky a different color, as though we stood at the very threshold of hell.\n\nThe road ahead was closed. We took no photographs and conducted no interviews. We simply turned back. And... fortunately, we made it back alive.",
+  "InfoDisplay_38010006_Title": "Newspaper Excerpt",
   "InfoDisplay_70050063_Text": "A photo secretly taken by the Plushie Squad. They witnessed your ascending to the top.",
   "InfoDisplay_70050063_Tittle": "Above the Stars",
   "InfoDisplay_70050065_Text": "Completed a month before the Blessed Maiden's coronation, this portrait has invited much discussion within the halls of Avinoleum. What, exactly, is the Blessed Maiden contemplating? Some believe she is lost in fond memories of the past; others argue she reflects on weightier matters like faith. A few suggest her thoughts go to the future of Ragunna, or all of Rinascita itself.\n\nWhatever the truth, perhaps only the Maiden herself holds the answer.",
